---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---
<SiteLayout
  title="Web emulation model"
  description="How December Reach acquires real OS source and performs emulation in a JavaScript backend."
  current="emulation"
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <p class="page-category">December Reach / Web emulation</p>
    <h1>The web is the way in.<br /><span>Not the machine.</span></h1>
    <p class="page-lead">
      December Reach uses the website to acquire and preload OS source. The
      actual emulation work belongs to the JavaScript backend, not to a
      browser-side CPU simulator.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section emulation-overview">
    <header class="document-heading">
      <span class="document-index">01</span>
      <h2>Three responsibilities</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="document-body">
      <p>Source preparation, system execution and web access are separate responsibilities. Keeping that boundary clear prevents the frontend from becoming a pretend emulator.</p>
      <div class="emulation-sequence">
        <article class="emulation-row">
          <span class="emulation-count">01</span>
          <div>
            <p class="emulation-role">Frontend</p>
            <h3>Source acquisition</h3>
            <p>The site obtains the operating-system source and preloads what is needed before an emulation session begins.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="emulation-row">
          <span class="emulation-count">02</span>
          <div>
            <p class="emulation-role">Backend</p>
            <h3>JavaScript emulation</h3>
            <p>The backend owns the emulation process and execution environment. CPU, memory and device behavior are not simulated by the website UI.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="emulation-row">
          <span class="emulation-count">03</span>
          <div>
            <p class="emulation-role">Web access</p>
            <h3>Reach the session</h3>
            <p>The browser is the access surface for the system. It is not the guest machine's execution host.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section">
    <header class="document-heading">
      <span class="document-index">02</span>
      <h2>Why the split matters</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="document-body">
      <p>The frontend and emulator are not interchangeable layers. The frontend is responsible for obtaining and preparing source. The JavaScript backend performs the emulation itself.</p>
      <p>This keeps the guest operating system's execution model out of presentation code and allows the emulation runtime to evolve independently from the website interface.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section">
    <header class="document-heading">
      <span class="document-index">03</span>
      <h2>Source repository</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="document-body">
      <p>The OS source collection is maintained separately from this website.</p>
      <a class="metro-link" href="https://github.com/DecemberReach/reach-to-be-reached" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        Open reach-to-be-reached on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <aside class="document-note">This repository contains the static Astro website. The JavaScript emulation service is a separate execution component and is not bundled into the GitHub Pages frontend.</aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
